# You should see AssertionError for the line `transfer(peter, susan, 100)`.
# This means there was not enough money in Peter's account to complete the transfer.
# You should see AssertionError for the line `transfer(susan, peter, 50)` as well.
# This means that Susan's total account balance did not increase after the transfer.
# This is due to the fact that the total balance of all accounts in Susan's and Peter's account
# is less than the amount of money we tried to transfer from Susan to Peter, which was 100+50=150.
# Trying to transfer 150 money from Susan's account to Peter's will cause an assertion to fail.
# This shows that the total balance of all accounts in a person can't decrease due to a transfer.
# In real application code, the total balance of all the accounts in a person/user should never decrease.
# and this will lead to the financial leak and inconsistency, if not handled. Therefore, in practical applications,
# such scenarios should be handled based on the specific requirements of the use-case at hand.
# in this case, if total_balance(User A) > money_to_transfer, then it is safe to transfer, otherwise not. Let's add that check into our function and it will work properly.
