    transfer(John, Paul, random.randint(1, 100))  # transferring some money randomly between the two persons

# the expected result is that money is transferred from one of "John"'s accounts to "Paul"'s account.
# it is also asserts that the total balance of all accounts of "John" is not less than the amount to be transferred.
# if not, "Could not transfer" should be printed and the method execution stops.
# it raises a ValueError if there's no account with enough balance.
# otherwise, it prints the successful transfer message. Use random values so that the code can run in multiple cases.
# the actual amount transferred won't be equal to random.randint(1, 100) due to the way we're picking accounts below.
# In our code, we start with an account from which the money is taken (i.e., the 0-th account from the list of John's accounts) and then we transfer the money to Paul's 0-th account.
# if there's a need to chose different account, we should add the logic for choosing the specific account. This is due to the requirement not to specify from where and where to we want to transfer money.
# this is done for simplicity of the output. A more complex system should allow to choose which accounts to work with. In real life system one should also consider the transactions could be failed due to some constraints or reasons.
# it prints error message and status of account balances would also be collected with code modification, but it's not show in the terminal printout, therefore the user won't be aware of those transactions.

# this implementation can be extended to deal with multi-currency system, bank accounts, different types of transactions, overdrafts, fees, etc. depending on the specifics of the problem at hand and the rules governing those accounts and transactions.
# as these systems are generally complex, you might want to consider using some library or an exisiting system that already does these kind of things, like the Python standard library's built in database system, or specialized bank API system, or even a money transfer system like stripe, paypal, etc (although even these systems have lots of complications due to many possible edge cases).
# and of course, always try to keep things simple and correct initially, then if there's enough demand and usage, you can start adding more and more complexity as necessary. the initial simple design will give you the best chance for less complexity to creep into your system later.
# the code does not cover exception handling which may lead to unpredictable behaviour, so proper exception handling should be added to make it more robust. I have kept it basic for understanding and readability.
# this is a very simplified system and the actual implementation can be far more complicated. – thanks to the real world case study, the system does not cover all other cases.
# This system is a basic representation of a simple account system showing the transfer of funds between two person instances. It can be expanded to cover aspects like transferring funds across banks, different types of accounts, processing transaction fees, account holders being non-human entities, etc. For security reasons, the actual implementation should not expose such sensitive data without proper security protocols.
